    "c9": {
      "anchor": "rs-care#markdown-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "7.5 Where RS needs care",
      "excerpt": "Behaviour-crisis notes: they go in the org's own private space, for the Board al",
      "text": "Think about marking documents as \"sensitive\" and encrypting the contents",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-02T03:00:16.694Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "Whoever runs the servers and the host can still read them, so RS should know that, or keep them elsewhere for now.",
      "offset": 85,
      "edited": "2026-11-02T03:00:43.352Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-02T03:12:08.015Z",
          "html": "<p>It's a good fit, because Spell and the host can't expose what they can't read, which is the same reasoning as never gating a read.  I'd do it later, not in the first version, so it's in \"Later\", and this note points there.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><b>Costs:</b>  Spell can't search it, project it or check what's inside, and losing the key loses the document.</li>\n  <li><b>The hard part is the keys:</b>  they have to reach everyone on a space's list, and change when someone comes off it.  AT Protocol doesn't provide this, so it's an epic of its own.</li>\n  <li><b>What it doesn't hide:</b>  that the document exists, who wrote it and when, and anything someone already read.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Until then, RS keeps its crisis notes elsewhere, or accepts that the host can read them.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-02T03:11:44.046Z"
      }
    },
